{
  "dom_id": "step-q75999",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250658/assets/c12deadd41a9c2853d0d2d50ba5c19ac6ca13012d7672941c92fa5e6cc5df642.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true for the graph shown above?\n\nI. $y=-1$ is a horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$\n\nII. $y=1$ is a horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $y=2$ is a horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's analyze each statement one-by-one.\n\n- On the right of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $1.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty \n$$\n\nThis means that $y=1$ is a horizontal asymptote of $f(x).$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- On the left of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $2.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 2\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to -\\infty .\n$$\n\nThis means that $y=2$ is a horizontal asymptote of $f(x).$ Therefore, III is also true.\n\n- The horizontal asymptotes of $f(x)$ are $y=1$ and $y=2.$ The line $y=-1$ is not a horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ because the function does not level off to $-1$ on the right nor on the left. Therefore, I is false.\n\nAs a result, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
